 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.independencia.03.lideres",
-  "title": "Gerundio: Bolívar y San Martín en paralelo",
+  "title": "The gerund: two things at once",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "When two campaigns run at once, the gerund lets a sentence pack in what someone was doing alongside the main action — organizing an army before crossing, descending unexpectedly, resigning surprisingly. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know the *gerund* for parallel and simultaneous actions from B1 Unit 2. The gerund says how or while the main verb happens, and the person is the same. It fits the campaigns of Bolívar and San Martín, which advanced while doing something else. The gerund does not change: *subiendo*, *venciendo*."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "San Martín se movía desde el sur, organizando un ejército en Argentina antes de cruzar los Andes.",
-          "english": "San Martín moved from the south, organizing an army in Argentina before crossing the Andes."
+          "spanish": "Los soldados cruzaron los Andes subiendo por caminos de montaña.",
+          "english": "The soldiers crossed the Andes going up mountain paths."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Ambas campañas, actuando de manera independiente pero con un objetivo compartido, convergerían finalmente en Perú.",
-          "english": "Both campaigns, acting independently but with a shared goal, would finally converge in Peru."
+          "spanish": "Bolívar avanzó por el norte venciendo a los realistas.",
+          "english": "Bolívar advanced through the north defeating the royalists."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Descendiendo inesperadamente sobre territorio neogranadino, sus fuerzas lograron una victoria decisiva en Boyacá.",
-          "english": "Descending unexpectedly onto New Granadan territory, his forces achieved a decisive victory at Boyacá."
+          "spanish": "San Martín preparó el ejército en Mendoza formando soldados.",
+          "english": "San Martín prepared the army in Mendoza training soldiers."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "San Martín, renunciando sorprendentemente a su cargo, se retiró por completo de la vida pública.",
-          "english": "San Martín, surprisingly resigning his post, withdrew completely from public life."
+          "spanish": "Los dos generales avanzaron desde direcciones opuestas, buscando el mismo objetivo.",
+          "english": "The two generals advanced from opposite directions, looking for the same aim."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Notice how the gerund can sit right next to the subject with commas on both sides (San Martín, *renunciando*..., *se retiró*) — this reads almost like an aside, adding a second fact about the subject without making it the main point of the sentence."
+      "content": "The gerund has the same subject as the main verb: *Bolívar avanzó venciendo* means Bolívar did both. The tense comes from the main verb only."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-independencia-04-republicas-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-independencia-04-republicas-gr.json
index ecbac7c8..d22b74d5 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-independencia-04-republicas-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-independencia-04-republicas-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.independencia.04.republicas",
-  "title": "Conectores formales: a raíz de, en el marco de",
+  "title": "Deber and tener que: what had to be done",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*A raíz de* introduces a cause ('as a result of'); en el marco de sets a broader context or framework something happened within ('within the context of'). Together they let a paragraph explain both why something happened and what larger situation it was part of. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know *tener que* and *deber* from B1 Unit 4: *tener que* says what you have to do, and *deber* says what you should do. The new republics had both kinds of problems. *Tuvieron que* (preterite) says what they had to do at one time; *tenían que* (imperfect) describes a duty that lasted."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "A raíz de más de una década de guerra casi continua, las nuevas repúblicas heredaron economías devastadas.",
-          "english": "As a result of more than a decade of almost continuous war, the new republics inherited devastated economies."
+          "spanish": "Los gobiernos tuvieron que pedir préstamos para pagar a los soldados.",
+          "english": "The governments had to ask for loans to pay the soldiers."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "En el marco de este colapso económico, varios gobiernos solicitaron préstamos a bancos británicos.",
-          "english": "Within the context of this economic collapse, several governments requested loans from British banks."
+          "spanish": "Los artesanos tenían que competir con productos baratos.",
+          "english": "The craftsmen had to compete with cheap products."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Esa burbuja, en el marco de la primera gran crisis financiera internacional moderna, se desplomó en 1825.",
-          "english": "That bubble, within the context of the first great modern international financial crisis, collapsed in 1825."
+          "spanish": "Un gobierno debe pensar en cómo va a pagar un préstamo.",
+          "english": "A government should think about how it will pay a loan."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "A raíz de la exclusión sistemática de los criollos de los puestos más altos, había relativamente poca gente con experiencia de gobierno.",
-          "english": "As a result of the systematic exclusion of criollos from the highest posts, there were relatively few people with governing experience."
+          "spanish": "Entonces tuvieron que renegociar las deudas.",
+          "english": "Then they had to renegotiate the debts."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "En el marco de doesn't claim direct causation the way *a raíz de* does — it just says 'this is the larger situation this fits inside.' Use it when you want to place an event in context without necessarily arguing that context caused it."
+      "content": "*Tener que* + infinitive is a real necessity (*tuvieron que pedir*); *deber* + infinitive is advice or duty (*debe pensar*). Do not add *que* after *deber*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-independencia-05-interpretacion-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-independencia-05-interpretacion-gr.json
index 02c9aba8..b718ddf8 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-independencia-05-interpretacion-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-independencia-05-interpretacion-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.independencia.05.interpretacion",
-  "title": "Voz pasiva: los beneficios desiguales de la independencia",
+  "title": "Aunque: promises and reality",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "A synthesis on who did and didn't benefit from independence needs passive voice constantly: a principle was proclaimed, a law was passed, a protection was eliminated, a right was not granted. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know *aunque* from A2 Unit 28: it admits a fact that goes against what follows. With a real fact, use the indicative. This pattern is useful when you compare what the constitutions said with what happened."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "En casi todas las nuevas constituciones, la ciudadanía fue proclamada igual para todos los hombres libres.",
-          "english": "In almost every new constitution, citizenship was proclaimed equal for all free men."
+          "spanish": "Aunque Chile abolió la esclavitud en 1823, en Brasil continuó hasta 1888.",
+          "english": "Although Chile abolished slavery in 1823, in Brazil it continued until 1888."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Fueron aprobadas leyes de 'vientre libre', que declaraban libres solo a los hijos nacidos después de cierta fecha.",
-          "english": "'Free womb' laws were passed, declaring free only children born after a certain date."
+          "spanish": "Aunque muchas mujeres lucharon en los ejércitos, no obtuvieron el voto.",
+          "english": "Although many women fought in the armies, they did not get the vote."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Esa protección fue eliminada por muchas de las nuevas repúblicas, inspiradas en ideas liberales.",
-          "english": "That protection was eliminated by many of the new republics, inspired by liberal ideas."
+          "spanish": "Aunque las constituciones hablaban de libertad, muchas personas quedaron fuera.",
+          "english": "Although the constitutions talked about freedom, many people were left out."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "El derecho al voto no fue concedido a las mujeres en ningún país latinoamericano en el momento de la independencia.",
-          "english": "The right to vote was not granted to women in any Latin American country at the time of independence."
+          "spanish": "Aunque la ley era igual para todos, no todos tenían los mismos recursos.",
+          "english": "Although the law was the same for all, not everyone had the same resources."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "A negative passive (no fue concedido) is just as useful as a positive one for this kind of synthesis — sometimes the most important historical fact is precisely what was NOT done, and passive voice states that just as cleanly as it states what was."
+      "content": "*Aunque* can open the sentence or follow the main clause, and it uses the indicative for a real, known fact. A comma separates a sentence-opening *aunque* clause."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-nuevasrepublicas-01-formacion-estado-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-nuevasrepublicas-01-formacion-estado-gr.json
index a806a7f6..5967a5c8 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-nuevasrepublicas-01-formacion-estado-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-nuevasrepublicas-01-formacion-estado-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.nuevasrepublicas.01.formacion-estado",
-  "title": "Se pasiva / impersonal: formación del Estado",
+  "title": "The passive with ser: what was created and dissolved",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "State-building is a natural fit for the se-passive: institutions were organized, authorities were established, an option was chosen, a federation was formed — a process too collective and improvised to credit to one person. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know the passive with *ser* + past participle from B1 Unit 18: *La ley fue aprobada por el Parlamento*. It puts the result in front, and it fits the history of new states, where the important thing is what was created, dissolved or defeated. The participle agrees with the subject: *creada*, *disuelta*, *derrotados*."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Después de 1821, se organizaron administraciones desde cero.",
-          "english": "After 1821, administrations were organized from scratch."
+          "spanish": "La Gran Colombia fue creada en 1819.",
+          "english": "The Gran Colombia was created in 1819."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Se establecieron autoridades locales y regionales en todo el nuevo territorio.",
-          "english": "Local and regional authorities were established across the new territory."
+          "spanish": "La federación centroamericana fue disuelta entre 1838 y 1841.",
+          "english": "The Central American federation was dissolved between 1838 and 1841."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "En Centroamérica se optó inicialmente por una solución federal.",
-          "english": "In Central America, a federal solution was initially chosen."
+          "spanish": "La Confederación Perú-Boliviana fue derrotada en 1839.",
+          "english": "The Peru–Bolivian Confederation was defeated in 1839."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "En 1823 se formaron las Provincias Unidas del Centro de América.",
-          "english": "In 1823, the United Provinces of Central America were formed."
+          "spanish": "Las primeras constituciones fueron escritas por congresos de cada país.",
+          "english": "The first constitutions were written by the congresses of each country."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "A whole paragraph of se-passive verbs in a row (*se organizaron*, *se establecieron*, *se optó*, *se formaron*) reads as a checklist of everything a new state had to figure out at once — useful when the text wants to convey how much was happening simultaneously, not in a tidy sequence."
+      "content": "Use *fue* with a singular subject and *fueron* with a plural one, and make the participle agree (*fue disuelta*, *fueron disueltos*). Add *por* + the agent when you want to name who did it; leave it out when it does not matter."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-nuevasrepublicas-02-territorio-fronteras-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-nuevasrepublicas-02-territorio-fronteras-gr.json
